            "Pain is 'whatever the experiencing person says it is, existing whenever they say it does' — a subjective experience that the nurse cannot measure from outside and must therefore believe. <strong>Nociceptive</strong> pain arises from tissue damage detected by nociceptors and carried by A-delta fibres (sharp, fast, well localised) and C fibres (dull, slow, aching) through the dorsal horn, where the <strong>gate control</strong> theory explains why rubbing, pressure, heat, cold, distraction and emotion can close the gate and reduce the pain that reaches the brain, and up the spinothalamic tract to the thalamus and cortex, where it is interpreted in the light of memory, mood, culture and meaning. <strong>Somatic</strong> nociceptive pain from skin, muscle, bone and joint is localised; <strong>visceral</strong> pain from organs is diffuse, cramping and <strong>referred</strong> to skin served by the same spinal segments — the heart to the left arm and jaw, the diaphragm to the shoulder tip, the gallbladder to the right scapula. <strong>Neuropathic</strong> pain arises from damage to the nerves themselves — burning, shooting, electric, with tingling, numbness and allodynia — in diabetic neuropathy, herpes zoster, HIV, sciatica, after amputation (phantom pain) and stroke, and it responds poorly to ordinary analgesics and better to the adjuvants. <strong>Acute</strong> pain is recent, has a cause, serves as a warning, activates the sympathetic system (tachycardia, hypertension, sweating, pallor, dilated pupils) and resolves with healing; <strong>chronic</strong> pain persists beyond three to six months, has lost its warning function, exhausts the sympathetic signs so that the patient looks 'comfortable' while in severe pain, and brings depression, sleeplessness, loss of work and relationships. <strong>Cancer pain</strong> and the <strong>pain of sickle cell crisis</strong> are the chronic-with-acute pains that Ghanaian nurses see most, and both are undertreated.",
