            "The <strong>WHO analgesic ladder</strong> treats mild pain (1–3) with a <strong>non-opioid</strong> — paracetamol 1 g four times a day to a maximum of 4 g (less in low body weight, liver disease, malnutrition and alcohol use), a non-steroidal such as ibuprofen or diclofenac with food, avoided in kidney disease, asthma that reacts, peptic ulcer, bleeding, heart failure and the third trimester — with or without an adjuvant; moderate pain (4–6) with a <strong>weak opioid</strong> — codeine, tramadol — added to the non-opioid; and severe pain (7–10) with a <strong>strong opioid</strong>, of which <strong>oral morphine</strong> is the first choice, titrated to effect with <strong>no ceiling dose</strong>, added to the non-opioid and the adjuvant. Its principles are <strong>by the mouth</strong> (oral wherever possible — intramuscular injections are painful, erratic and unnecessary for most pain), <strong>by the clock</strong> (regular doses for continuous pain, because waiting for pain to return before the next dose means chasing it all day), <strong>by the ladder</strong> (up when not controlled, down as it settles), <strong>for the individual</strong> (the right dose is the one that relieves this patient's pain with tolerable side effects) and <strong>with attention to detail</strong> — a written plan the patient can read, a <strong>breakthrough dose</strong> of about one-sixth of the 24-hour morphine dose available for pain between regular doses, a <strong>laxative</strong> prescribed with every opioid from the first dose, an anti-emetic for the nausea of the first days, and a review every day. <strong>Severe acute pain starts at step 3</strong>: a fractured femur, a sickle cell crisis, a burn, a myocardial infarction and post-operative pain are not made to climb from paracetamol, and the first doses are intravenous and titrated — small increments every five minutes until the pain is controlled, with the sedation and the respiratory rate watched. <strong>Adjuvants</strong> treat the pain that opioids treat badly: amitriptyline, gabapentin, pregabalin and carbamazepine for neuropathic pain; dexamethasone for nerve compression, bone pain and raised intracranial pressure; hyoscine butylbromide for colic; diazepam or baclofen for muscle spasm; bisphosphonates for bone metastases; and local anaesthetic blocks, epidurals and patient-controlled analgesia where the facility can provide them.",
