            "Heat and cold are the oldest analgesics and still burn and freeze patients every year. <strong>Heat</strong> — a hot water bottle filled two-thirds with water at about 40–46 °C (hotter from the kettle must be allowed to cool), the air expelled, the stopper checked and the bottle <strong>wrapped in a cover</strong>; a warm moist compress; a heat pad; a <strong>sitz bath</strong> for the perineum after delivery or haemorrhoidectomy at 40–43 °C for fifteen to twenty minutes; or warm soaks — dilates vessels, relaxes muscle, raises the pain threshold and localises an abscess, and is applied for <strong>fifteen to twenty minutes</strong> at a time because prolonged heat produces a rebound vasoconstriction and the skin's tolerance falls. It is <strong>not applied</strong> in the first twenty-four to forty-eight hours after an injury (it increases bleeding and swelling), over bleeding, over an undiagnosed abdomen (an inflamed appendix heated to comfort can rupture), over malignancy, or to a patient who <strong>cannot feel or report a burn</strong> — the diabetic with neuropathy, the paralysed, the unconscious, the sedated, the confused, the infant and the very old — in whom, if heat is used at all, it is at a lower temperature, checked every five minutes and never left unattended; the hot water bottle placed against the feet of an unconscious or a diabetic patient is a classic negligence case. <strong>Cold</strong> — an ice pack or frozen peas <strong>wrapped in a towel</strong>, a cold compress, cool running water — constricts vessels, limits bleeding and swelling, numbs and reduces spasm, and is the treatment of the first forty-eight hours after a sprain, strain or bruise (with rest, compression and elevation), of a nosebleed, of a minor burn (<strong>cool running water for twenty minutes</strong>, not ice, which deepens the burn) and of insect stings; it is applied for fifteen to twenty minutes and removed when the skin is numb, and it is <strong>not applied</strong> to open wounds, to the limbs of a patient with peripheral vascular disease or Raynaud's, to a patient with <strong>sickle cell disease</strong>, in whom cold precipitates a crisis, to areas of reduced sensation, or to a febrile patient who is shivering. In both, the skin is inspected before, during and after, and the application, the temperature, the duration, the skin findings and the effect on pain are recorded.",
