            "A <strong>generalised tonic-clonic seizure</strong> — epilepsy, febrile convulsion in a child, cerebral malaria, meningitis, eclampsia, hypoglycaemia, head injury, alcohol withdrawal, hyponatraemia, poisoning — has a tonic phase of stiffening with a cry, apnoea and cyanosis, a clonic phase of rhythmic jerking with frothing and often incontinence and tongue-biting, and a post-ictal phase of deep sleep, confusion and headache. <strong>During</strong> the seizure the nurse <strong>notes the time</strong>, stays with the patient, calls for help, protects the head with a pillow or her hands and the body from hard and sharp objects, loosens tight clothing at the neck, removes spectacles, <strong>does not restrain</strong> the limbs (which dislocates and fractures), <strong>puts nothing in the mouth</strong> — no spoon, no cloth, no fingers; the tongue cannot be swallowed and teeth and fingers are broken — does not give anything by mouth, turns the patient onto the side as soon as the jerking allows so that the airway drains, gives oxygen if available, and <strong>observes</strong>: the time of onset and the duration, where the movement started and how it spread, the eyes, the colour, incontinence, injury, and the breathing. A seizure that lasts <strong>more than five minutes</strong>, or seizures that follow each other without recovery, is <strong>status epilepticus</strong>, an emergency: the doctor is called urgently, the blood glucose is checked, and the prescribed benzodiazepine — intravenous or rectal diazepam, buccal or intramuscular midazolam — is given according to the protocol, with the airway, breathing and saturation supported; in a pregnant woman with hypertension the drug is magnesium sulphate and the diagnosis is eclampsia until proved otherwise.",
