        "Pain is whatever the patient says it is: nociceptive somatic and visceral pain with its referral patterns, neuropathic pain that needs adjuvants, acute pain with sympathetic signs and chronic pain without; it is assessed with PQRST and a scale fitted to the patient — numerical, FACES, FLACC, Abbey or PAINAD — at every set of observations and again after each intervention, and the myths that leave it untreated are refused.",
        "The WHO ladder gives paracetamol and non-steroidals for mild pain, a weak opioid for moderate and oral morphine without ceiling for severe, by the mouth, by the clock, by the ladder, for the individual and with attention to detail — a breakthrough dose of one-sixth, a laxative with every opioid, adjuvants for neuropathic pain — and severe acute pain starts at the top step with intravenous titration.",
        "Opioid safety rests on the sedation score and the respiratory rate before and at the peak of each dose, naloxone titrated in small repeated doses, anticipation of constipation and nausea, and the knowledge that tolerance and dependence are not addiction; in sickle cell crisis the patient is believed and the dose that worked last time is the starting point, and non-pharmacological measures close the gate alongside.",
        "Heat at 40–46 °C, covered, for fifteen to twenty minutes relieves spasm and chronic pain but is withheld in the first two days after injury, over an undiagnosed abdomen and from patients who cannot feel a burn; cold, wrapped, for fifteen to twenty minutes limits bleeding and swelling in the first forty-eight hours but is withheld from open wounds, poor circulation and sickle cell disease, and a burn is cooled with running water, not ice.",
        "The tepid sponge follows an antipyretic, uses water at 27–34 °C with wet flannels in the axillae and groins, and stops when the temperature has fallen a degree or to 38 °C or the patient shivers; cold water, ice and alcohol are never used.",
        "The unconscious patient is nursed system by system — recovery position with suction and nothing by mouth, breathing and circulation monitored with thrombosis prevention and no hot applications, the Glasgow Coma Scale by component, two-hourly turns, eyes closed and lubricated, mouth care two- to four-hourly, nasogastric feeding with a pH check and the head up, a catheter bundle and a bowel programme, passive exercises twice daily — with every word spoken as though heard and the family informed, involved and supported.",
        "During a seizure the nurse times it, protects the head, restrains nothing, puts nothing in the mouth, turns the patient to the side as soon as possible and observes in detail; after five minutes it is status epilepticus needing a glucose check and the protocol benzodiazepine (or magnesium in eclampsia); afterwards the recovery position, observations, glucose, injury check and nil by mouth until awake, with seizure precautions, anticonvulsants on time and teaching that epilepsy is an illness and not a curse.",
