          alt: "Triage in a mass-casualty incident: sorting by who benefits most. Four categories. Red — immediate: life-threatening but salvageable — airway obstruction, severe bleeding, shock, tension pneumothorax, severe burns to the face or airway; treat now. Yellow — urgent: serious but stable for the moment — fractures without shock, moderate burns, abdominal injuries with normal pulse; treat within an hour. Green — delayed, the walking wounded: minor wounds, sprains, small burns; can walk — direct them to a holding area, treat last. Black — dead or unsalvageable: not breathing after the airway is opened, injuries incompatible with life; do not start CPR in a mass-casualty incident — the rescuers are needed by the red. The sieve: can they walk? — green. Not walking — breathing? no breathing after airway opened — black; breathing rate over 30 or under 10 — red; capillary refill over 2 seconds or pulse over 120 — red; otherwise — yellow. Tag every casualty; re-triage repeatedly — people move between colours; one person commands, one triages, treatment follows triage, transport goes red first. The quiet casualty is checked before the noisy one.",
          caption: "Walkers are green; no breathing after opening the airway is black; a rate over 30 or under 10, a refill over two seconds or a pulse over 120 is red; the rest are yellow. Tag them, re-triage them, and send the red to the vehicles first — the quiet casualty is checked before the screaming one.",
